<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Forbes Burnham</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1923">1923</a>–<a href="/years/1985">1985</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Linden Forbes Sampson Burnham</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Forbes Burnham was a lawyer who co-founded the People's Progressive Party with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheddi-jagan">Cheddi Jagan</a>, then broke away to lead the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-national-congress">People's National Congress</a>, drawing support mainly from African Guyanese. He became premier of British Guiana in 1964 and led Guyana to independence in 1966. He declared the Co-operative Republic in 1970, nationalised the bauxite and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> industries, and was executive president from 1980 until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/forbes-burnham.jpg" alt="Forbes Burnham" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fotograaf Onbekend / Anefo · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Forbes_Burnham_(1966).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
